---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="09-recursion"
  lessonSlug="00-example-recursive-countdown"
  hook="The textbook's first recursive function. Same output as the Chapter 3 while-loop version, different shape."
>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def countdown(n):
    if n <= 0:
        print("Blastoff!")
    else:
        print(n)
        countdown(n - 1)        # ← function calls itself

countdown(3)`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="Source: Think Python §5.7">
    <p>
      <em>"It is legal for one function to call another; it is also legal
      for a function to call itself. It may not be obvious why that is a
      good thing, but it turns out to be one of the most magical things a
      program can do."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Every recursive function has two pieces</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Base case</strong> — <code>if n &lt;= 0: print("Blastoff!")</code> — the "stop" condition.</li>
    <li><strong>Recursive case</strong> — <code>countdown(n - 1)</code> — call self with a smaller / closer-to-base argument.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why <code>&lt;= 0</code>, not <code>== 0</code>?</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="Don't actually run this — it will RecursionError"
    code={`def countdown_BUG(n):
    if n == 0:
        print("Blastoff!")
    else:
        print(n)
        countdown_BUG(n - 1)

# countdown_BUG(-1)   # → RecursionError: -1, -2, -3, ... never hits 0
print("(skipped — would RecursionError)")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="From TP §5.10">
    <p>
      Every recursive call must get <strong>strictly closer</strong> to the
      base case. <code>n &lt;= 0</code> catches any non-positive input.
      <code>n == 0</code> only catches exactly zero — negative inputs
      recurse forever.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
